# Searching by permissions — security critical!
find /etc -perm 644
Find files with EXACT permission 644 (rw-r--r--). Octal notation. Exact match — must be exactly 644!

find / -perm /4000
Find files where ANY of the specified bits are set. /4000 = any file with SUID bit set. SECURITY AUDIT! Find all SUID files.

find / -perm -4000
Find files where ALL specified bits are set. -4000 = SUID bit definitely set (other bits can vary). Most common for SUID search!

find / -perm -4000 -type f 2>/dev/null
Find ALL SUID files on system. Security audit! SUID = runs as file OWNER not caller. Should be minimal. Unexpected SUID = security risk!

find / -perm -2000 -type f 2>/dev/null
Find ALL SGID files. 2000 = SGID bit. Review what has SGID set — should match known list!

find / -perm -o+w -type f 2>/dev/null
Find WORLD-WRITABLE files. Anyone can write to these! Security risk — should not exist outside /tmp! Audit regularly.

find / -perm -o+w -type d 2>/dev/null
Find world-writable DIRECTORIES. /tmp is expected. Others are security risks! Check immediately.

find /var/www -perm 000
Find files with NO permissions (000). These are inaccessible to everyone! Usually misconfigured — fix with chmod.
